  "reasoning": "The patient reports skin peeling and rashes on the arms, legs, and back, accompanied by joint pain. This combination of dermatological symptoms (scaly, peeling plaques) and musculoskeletal symptoms (joint pain) is a classic presentation of psoriasis, which is frequently associated with psoriatic arthritis. Reviewing the training references, References [1] and [7] describe patients with nearly identical symptoms—rashes, dry/scaly/peeling skin on the extremities and torso, and joint pain—and both yield a diagnosis of psoriasis. While arthritis explains the joint pain, it does not account for the skin peeling and rashes. Drug reactions and fungal infections can cause rashes but typically do not present with this specific combination of peeling skin and joint pain. Therefore, psoriasis is the most factually supported diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]psoriasis[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
